Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python script that groups the subdirectories of the current working directory
into a fixed number of balanced folders, measuring each subdirectory's recursive
file size and distributing them using a greedy size-balancing strategy.
The prompt should specify: Purpose: Organize the immediate subdirectories of the
current directory into N balanced groups (N supplied as a command-line
argument), so that the total size of each group is roughly equal, by moving each
subdirectory into a newly created group folder.
Inputs: - A required positive integer command-line argument specifying the
number of parts/groups, parsed with argparse. - The current working directory is
the implicit input; no other files are read.
Outputs: - Console output: the count of discovered subdirectories, a
per-subdirectory size line in megabytes with two decimal places, and a final
confirmation message after reorganization. - Filesystem changes: creation of
group folders named with a "group_" prefix followed by a 1-based index, and
moving each subdirectory into the group folder assigned to it.
Required behavior and logic: - Only consider immediate child directories of the
current directory (not files, not nested directories); exclude any directory
whose name already starts with "group_" so that re-runs do not regroup previous
output. - Compute each directory's total size by recursively walking all files
beneath it (rglob), summing file sizes via stat, skipping files that raise
PermissionError or FileNotFoundError, and tolerating errors at the top-level
walk without crashing.
Do not follow the sizes of symlinks as files beyond what is_file reports. - Sort
directories by size in descending order, then assign each to the group with the
currently smallest accedy longest-processing-time bin packing). - Create each
group folder (with exist_ok-style behavior so existing folders are reused) and
move each assigned subdirectory into it using shutil.move. - If no
subdirectories are found, print a message stating so and exit without changes. -
Handle edge cases: empty directories (size 0), name collisions when moving
(decide and document behavior, e.g., skip or report on existing destination),
permission errors during moves (report and continue), and a requested number of
parts that is zero or negative (reject with an error message via argparse
validation).
Execution details: - Use only the Python standard library: os, shutil, pathlib,
argparse. - Include a shebang line for Termux on Android
(/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python). - Provide a main entry point
guarded by a __name__ check that parses arguments and calls the grouping
function. - Use type hints on function signatures and keep the script a single
file with clear, minimal helper functions (one for directory size, one for the
grouping logic). - Print progress and sizes using f-strings with the size
converted to megabytes (bytes divided by 1024 times 1024).
Produce a complete, runnable script that follows these specifications. ---
